import type { Pattern } from '$lib/types';

// The particle, intonation, verb-form and word-order rows follow the single
// value WALS chapter 116 (Dryer 2013) gives each language, with the particle's
// position from chapter 92. A row that names the particle rests on the entry
// cited beside it.
//
// The A-not-A rows follow Grambank feature GB297, “Can polar interrogation be
// indicated by a V-not-V construction?”, and name the grammar its coders cite.
// Grambank accepts a word for “or” between the two verbs (Paamese); Luo (2013)
// and Wada (2026) count only the bare juxtaposition. Further rows come from
// Luo's survey of 138 languages of China, from Miestamo (2011), from APiCS
// feature 103 and from the grammars quoted in the examples. Where such a row
// stands against a Grambank coding of absence (Tibetan, Mon, Skolt Saami,
// Komi-Zyrian), its citations say so.
//
// Burmese has no A-not-A row. Grambank codes it from Luo (2013: 93), a page on
// the Burmish languages of China, and Luo (2013: 292) reports Burmese itself
// without the construction.
//
// Two WALS values fall outside the six strategies and have no row: Kabardian
// (a question drops the declarative morpheme) and Chalcatongo Mixtec (no
// difference between question and statement).

